    "answer": {
      "short": "$(x+y)^2$, given $x^2-y^2=1$.\n\nUse $x-y=\\frac{1}{x+y}$ after clearing denominators.",
      "worked": "Since $(x-y)(x+y)=1$, we have $x-y=\\dfrac1{x+y}$. Multiply top and bottom by $x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1+\\frac yx}{1-\\frac yx}&=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\\\\\frac{1+\\frac yx}{1-\\frac yx}&=\\frac{x+y}{\\frac1{x+y}}\\\\\\frac{1+\\frac yx}{1-\\frac yx}&=(x+y)^2\\end{align*}$$\nOriginal domain: real $x,y$ with $x\\ne0$ and $x\\ne y$, subject to $x^2-y^2=1$. The given condition already ensures these exclusions and $x+y\\ne0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
